(Discrepancy 7: LANGUAGE.md:469-476 is headed "Two Canonical Forms" and
 describes the no-initializer form only under `Create`. There are more forms
 than two: the lead-in may be absent, `Set` or `Create`, and the initializer
 may be `is V`, `to V` (after Set/Create only) or absent — and the entirely
 undocumented `a TYPE called NAME.` below works and takes the default. The
 two spellings are also not diagnostically equal: `a buffer called NAME.`
 emits a declaration warning on stderr at compile time while the documented
 `Create a buffer called NAME.` emits none, for the same resulting buffer.
 (The compile-time warning is not part of the recorded run output below; run
 the compiler on this file to see it.)
